From: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] chansession: mark the pty master fd as bidirectional

The pty master is a single fd used for both reading and writing, but
ptycommand() marked the channel as non-bidirectional (bidir_fd = 0).

When the client sends CHANNEL_EOF (which OpenSSH does immediately when
its stdin is already at EOF, e.g. `ssh -tt host cmd` from a non-tty),
check_close() calls close_chan_fd() on channel->writefd. With
bidir_fd = 0 that performs a real close() of the pty master, which hangs
up the slave and unlinks /dev/pts/N. That both breaks the session I/O and
races with the session child, which may not have opened /dev/pts/N yet:

  ioctl(TIOCSCTTY): Input/output error
  /dev/pts/0: No such file or directory
  open /dev/tty failed - could not set controlling tty: No such device or address

Marking the channel bidirectional (as other channels that share one fd
for both directions do) makes close_chan_fd() use shutdown(), which is a
no-op on a pty, so a client EOF no longer tears down the master. The
master is still closed at teardown when all channel fds are marked
closed.

Signed-off-by: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
---
 src/svr-chansession.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/svr-chansession.c b/src/svr-chansession.c
--- a/src/svr-chansession.c
+++ b/src/svr-chansession.c
@@ -927,7 +927,7 @@
 		channel->readfd = chansess->master;
 		/* don't need to set stderr here */
 		ses.maxfd = MAX(ses.maxfd, chansess->master);
-		channel->bidir_fd = 0;
+		channel->bidir_fd = 1;
 
 		setnonblocking(chansess->master);
 
